  "query_preview": "..\nKEY MESSAGE 1...\nZero precipitation cold front will be south of the CWA this \nevening/tonight, with only anafrontal cloud cover to show for it and \nwind shifts. As high pressure centered in Quebec builds back into \nthe region for Tuesday, winds out of the northeast will have the \nmost significant effect on temperatures, with the lakeshore areas \ndown several degrees into the low to mid 60s. Otherwise, temperature \ndecreases further inland in the wake of the cold front will be \nminimal. \n\nAnother cold front moves in from the northwest for late Thursday \ninto Thursday night. As mentioned above, the column RH is low, and \nwith the main energy of the low pressure system moving through \nnorthern Ontario and central Quebec, shower chances will be on the \ndecrease as the front moves into Ohio. Ultimately, chances will be \nmainly isolated, and only bare minimum QPF is expected, primarily in \nNE OH/NW PA. It will not be until the weekend when a pattern change \nbrings more of a spring-like pattern with a warm sector developing \nand a feed of moisture form the Gulf. This will increase convective \nchance going into the latter half of the weekend. Until that point \nin time, expect above normal temperatures from Wednesday onward, and \nwidespread 70s for this late October time frame.",
